1
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:12,370
When Microsoft bought task management app Wunderlist and mobile calendar Sunrise in 2015, it picked up two newcomers that were attracting considerable buzz in Silicon Valley.
2015年，微软收购了任务管理应用Wunderlist和移动日历Sunrise，将两家当时在硅谷颇受热议的新秀收入囊中。

2
00:00:13,170 --> 00:00:23,650
Microsoft’s own Office dominates the market for “productivity” software, but the start-ups represented a new wave of technology designed from the ground up for the smartphone world.
微软自家的Office主导着“生产力”软件市场，而这两家初创公司代表的是专为智能手机时代从零设计的新一波技术。

3
00:00:24,450 --> 00:00:31,200
Both apps, however, were later scrapped after Microsoft said it had used their best features in its own products.
然而，后来微软表示，已把这两款应用最好的功能用到了自家产品中，随后便将二者双双弃用。

4
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:40,650
Their teams of engineers stayed on, making them two of the many “acqui-hires” that the biggest companies have used to feed their great hunger for tech talent.
两家公司的工程师团队则留了下来，这样一来，这两笔收购就成了众多“人才收购”中的两例，而最大的几家公司一直靠这类收购来满足自己对科技人才的强烈渴求。

5
00:00:41,450 --> 00:00:51,770
To Microsoft’s critics, the fates of Wunderlist and Sunrise are examples of a remorseless drive by Big Tech to chew up any innovative companies that lie in their path.
在微软的批评者看来，Wunderlist和Sunrise的下场正是例证，说明科技巨头一心要无情地吞掉挡在自己路上的任何创新公司。

6
00:00:52,570 --> 00:01:04,650
“They bought the seedlings and closed them down,” complained Paul Arnold, a partner at San Francisco-based Switch Ventures, referring to deals that put an end to businesses that might one day turn into competitors.
“他们买下幼苗，然后就把它们关掉了。”总部位于旧金山的Switch Ventures公司合伙人保罗·阿诺德抱怨道。他所指的这些交易，让一些有朝一日可能成长为竞争对手的企业就此夭折。

7
00:01:05,450 --> 00:01:07,340
Microsoft declined to comment.
微软拒绝置评。

8
00:01:08,140 --> 00:01:22,660
As with other start-up investors, Mr Arnold’s own business often depends on selling start-ups to larger tech companies, though he admits to mixed feelings about the result: “I think these things are good for me, if I put my selfish hat on.
和其他初创公司投资人一样，阿诺德自己的生意也常常要靠把初创公司卖给更大的科技公司，不过他承认，自己对这种结果心情复杂：“要是从自私的角度考虑，我觉得这些事对我有好处。

9
00:01:23,460 --> 00:01:25,710
But are they good for the American economy?
但它们对美国经济有好处吗？

10
00:01:26,510 --> 00:01:27,380
I don’t know.”
我不知道。”

11
00:01:28,180 --> 00:01:32,650
The US Federal Trade Commission says it wants to find the answer to that question.
美国联邦贸易委员会表示，希望找到这个问题的答案。

12
00:01:33,450 --> 00:01:42,230
This week, it asked the five most valuable US tech companies for information about their many small acquisitions over the past decade.
本周，该委员会要求美国市值最高的五家科技公司提供各自过去十年间众多小规模收购的相关信息。

13
00:01:43,030 --> 00:01:53,510
Although only a research project at this stage, the request has raised the prospect of regulators wading into early-stage tech markets that until now have been beyond their reach.
尽管现阶段这只是一个研究项目，但这一要求已让人想到一种可能：监管机构会介入早期科技市场，而迄今为止，它们对这些市场一直鞭长莫及。

14
00:01:54,310 --> 00:02:06,680
Given their combined market value of more than $5.5 trillion, rifling through such small deals—many of them much less prominent than Wunderlist and Sunrise—might seem beside the point.
鉴于这五家公司的总市值超过5.5万亿美元，翻查这类小交易可能显得无关紧要——其中许多交易远不如Wunderlist和Sunrise那样引人注目。

15
00:02:07,480 --> 00:02:29,090
Between them, the five biggest tech companies have spent an average of only $3.4 billion a year on sub-$1 billion acquisitions over the past five years—a drop in the ocean compared with their massive financial reserves, and the more than $130 billion of venture capital that was invested in the US last year.
过去五年间，最大的五家科技公司在金额低于10亿美元的收购上合计平均每年只花了34亿美元——与它们庞大的资金储备相比，与去年在美国投入的1300多亿美元风险资本相比，这都不过是沧海一粟。

16
00:02:29,890 --> 00:02:43,530
However, critics say the big companies use such deals to buy their most threatening potential competitors before their businesses have a chance to gain momentum, in some cases as part of a “buy and kill” tactic to simply close them down.
然而，批评者说，大公司利用这类交易，趁最具威胁的潜在竞争对手的业务还没来得及发展壮大，就把它们买下来；有时这是“收购再扼杀”策略的一部分，目的就是干脆把它们关掉。
